            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="COL01" sex="1">
                    <persName>
                        <forename>Samuel Taylor</forename>
                        <surname>Coleridge</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1772-10-21">
                        <placeName> </placeName>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1834-07-25"/>
                    <occupation>poet</occupation>
                    <occupation>critic</occupation>
                    <occupation>philosopher</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Coleridge was one of the most eminent men of letters of his day. He met Godwin in 1794 but their friendship began in earnest in 1799. He advised Godwin 
      on some of his dramatic writings, mostly notably <title>Abbas, King of Persia</title> (1801). Godwin attended many of his lectures in 1808, 1811, and 1818-19. 
      Godwin considered him one of his 'four principal oral instructors', along with <persName key="FAW01">Joseph Fawcett</persName>, 
      <persName key="HOL01">Thomas Holcroft</persName>, and <persName key="DYS01">George Dyson</persName>.</p>
                        <p>Godwin recorded his death.</p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="MS">MS. Abinger c. 32, fol. 34: not dated</bibl>
